TITLE:: SFPlayerMeter
summary:: Graphical feedback window showing SFPlayer input levels and server output levels
categories:: GUI>Interfaces
related:: Classes/Stethoscope, Classes/FreqScope, Classes/ServerMeterView, Classes/SFPlayerMeterView

DESCRIPTION::
A SFPlayerMeter is a unified meter widget that combines link::Classes/ServerMeter2View:: for output monitoring with custom input meters for SFPlayer audio. It uses a hybrid approach: ServerMeter2View handles server outputs while custom LevelIndicator widgets monitor a specific player bus. This allows you to monitor the actual audio from SFPlayer rather than line inputs.

Also see:
link::Classes/Server#-meter::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
Create a new SFPlayerMeter.

ARGUMENT:: server
The link::Classes/Server:: whose outputs you wish to visualize.

ARGUMENT:: parent
The parent link::Classes/View:: or link::Classes/Window:: where the new SFPlayerMeter will be embedded.

ARGUMENT:: leftUp
Where to position the new SFPlayerMeter inside the parent. strong::leftUp:: must be a link::Classes/Point::, describing where to place the upper left corner.

ARGUMENT:: numIns
The number of inputs you want to display (typically 2 for stereo player audio)

ARGUMENT:: numOuts
The number of outputs you want to display (starts counting at bus 0)

ARGUMENT:: playerBus
The link::Classes/Bus:: where SFPlayer outputs its audio

returns:: A reference to the newly created SFPlayerMeter

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: meterView
Get a reference to the link::Classes/SFPlayerMeterView:: of this SFPlayerMeter

returns:: a link::Classes/SFPlayerMeterView:: reference

METHOD:: remove
Removes the meter from its parent view and cleans up all associated resources.

returns:: this SFPlayerMeter

note::
A SFPlayerMeter encapsulates a link::Classes/SFPlayerMeterView:: within a parent view. For more information about views see link::Guides/GUI-Introduction#Basic elements: Windows, views and containers::
::

EXAMPLES::

code::

s = Server.internal; // use the internal server
s.boot;

// Create a player bus and SFPlayer
~playerBus = Bus.audio(s, 2);
~sfp = SFPlayer.new("path/to/audio.wav", outbus: ~playerBus.index);

// Create a parent view for the meter
~meterContainer = View.new(Rect(0, 0, 300, 200));

// Display 2 player input channels & main stereo output
m = SFPlayerMeter.new(s, ~meterContainer, 0@0, 2, 2, ~playerBus);
::
